[ ] Export sandbox telemetry with standard attributes =sandbox-telemetry $Sessions export OTLP to the NUC collector, which writes agent-logs, agent-metrics and agent-traces jsonl under ~/.local/state/otelcol. From a container, the collector answers on the tailscale address, not host.containers.internal. Attributes, standard only: service.name and service.version from the harness itself, host.name, deployment.environment.name sandbox or interactive, vcs.repository.name, vcs.ref.head.name, vcs.ref.head.revision, cicd.pipeline.name agent-sandbox, cicd.pipeline.run.id for the run and cicd.pipeline.task.run.id per session. No clearhead attributes: runs join to actions through commits. The driver also timestamps build and each session in the manifest. Write the convention, with this table and its reasons, to docs/telemetry.md; interactive harness configs adopt host.name and deployment.environment.name. Sequence after the run-kind/driver boundary and automatic reconciliation stabilize: export meaningful terminal outcomes and systemd resource events, rather than instrumenting the current mixed-policy launcher twice. 2026-09-30: this is the observability half of the review feedback loop; the other half is the review and human-verdict records in the manifests, joined by run id.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2dd5-7753-9f1d-25c1a3aea18a
[ ] Query sandbox runs with duckdb =sandbox-benchmarks $A saved duckdb query file over ~/agent-runs manifests and the collector jsonl, answering: cost and time per outcome; build time against agent time; tool calls and gate runs per session; sandbox against interactive sessions. Questions first, no dashboard. Do after sandbox-telemetry. ADDED 2026-09-30: calibration questions from the review feedback loop: per reviewer model and kind of change, the miss rate (human 'missed' entries) and false-alarm rate (overruled findings), and whether transcript coverage predicts misses.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 <01a0dab3-2dd5-7753-9f1d-25c1a3aea18a #01a0dab3-2df6-7862-8b3a-df005de98823
[-] Add review runs and harness selection =sandbox-cross-vendor-review $RESOLVED by option A (orchestrator, 2026-09-30). Previous NEEDS DECISION: What transcript evidence counts as a changed file actually read, especially for shell-based review, and is conservative undercounting acceptable?
Evidence: agents/session:81-97 retains pi message_end events and Claude stream-json; scripts/agent-result:43-49 currently returns records without a coverage metric. Both harnesses allow shell tools. A shell argument mentioning a file (grep, git diff, cat, a pipeline, or a command that fails) is not proof its contents reached the reviewer; scanning command strings would let the model inflate the required metric. Counting only successful structured file-read tool results is auditable but excludes shell-based reads and repository diffs, including the grep-heavy review that motivated this action. Partial reads and deleted files also need a stated coverage meaning.
Options: (A) count only successful structured read results, label the metric instrumented-read coverage and explicitly accept undercounting; (B) add host-controlled read instrumentation for shell/diff reads, a scope expansion requiring a defined evidence contract; (C) report tool-observed file access as a heuristic with limitations, relaxing the actually-read requirement. Recommend A if a conservative lower bound is the goal, B if coverage must include shell reviews. No implementation changes made; stopped at this decision point under the Work run rules. Original decision follows unchanged.

DECIDED 2026-09-25 with the human: work and review are separate run kinds, never bundled; deciding which to run is orchestration, kept outside both. A work run takes an action or prompt and mounts its clone read-write; its output is commits and a closing message. A review run takes a finished run's branch, refs/agent/base to agent/id, mounts that clone read-only so report-without-fixing is enforced, and writes findings to its manifest; no commits. Every run picks its harness, claude or pi, through an adapter taking a prompt file and a transcript path and reporting result and cost; a review usually uses the other vendor. The work prompt drops its self-review step, and the runbook's review gate moves to between a work run and landing. pi: headless via pi -p --mode json, final answer from the last message_end event, cost summed from usage.cost; image pins pi 0.85.1; the sandbox's own ChatGPT/Codex login lives in the agent-pi volume, OpenRouter as fallback via the openrouter_token secret; the subscription quota is shared with interactive work. agent-run shares the host pi settings minus pi-background-tasks, remote-pi and pi-memory, and the host agents directory, read-only.  LESSONS 2026-09-25 from a manual pi work run and two pi reviews, for the adapter: pi exits 0 even when its session ends in error, so read the last assistant message_end stopReason; the final text is in message_end content; cost sums message_end usage.cost.total. Take the outcome from the action's real state in read actions, which includes completed actions; an action missing from it is not completed. Exclude pi-mcp-adapter too, or pi loads the repo's own .mcp.json. The adapter must harvest like agent-run: an unharvested manual run lost its commits to agent-land, now guarded. Codex once hit WebSocket 1011 and then an invalid-key error on retry; a rerun succeeded. LEARNED: independent patch-only no-tool reviews missed numeric systemd properties, a missing-anchor reminder, and launcher-interruption behavior; host smoke plus an actual read-only branch checkout caught these. Make review a first-class run kind with the finished branch mounted read-only and access to the action decision/specs, full diff and validation evidence. Emit a durable verdict and findings; do not mutate reviewed files, choose a different vendor from the worker, and require explicit reconciliation of blocking findings before landing. NARROWED 2026-09-30 with the human, after reading a review transcript: the reviewer did real work (15 tool calls, every caller grepped) but its closing message dropped the evidence, so 'land, no findings' could not be told apart from 'did not check'. What is left: the Review run section asks for a closing fenced JSON block {coverage: \[{check, how}], findings: \[{severity, file, line, what, why}], for_human: \[where judgment is needed], verdict}; the host parses it into the session record as review; agent-result adds a transcript-derived coverage count (changed files the session actually read, against the files the change touched), which the model cannot inflate; agent-harvest prints coverage, findings and for_human. A verdict informs landing and never gates it: agent-land prints unreconciled blocking findings and the human decides. Blocking findings go to a fixer session in the same workspace. DECISION (orchestrator, 2026-09-30): option A. Count only changed files opened in full by successful structured file-read tool results (pi read, Claude Code Read), for both transcript formats. Report opened_in_full: {read: \[...], changed: N} as a labeled lower bound, never reviewed. Shell/diff reads are excluded and belong in reviewer coverage. B host instrumentation and C shell-string parsing rejected. Implementing the remaining NARROWED scope. IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-30: Review run closes with validated fenced JSON coverage/findings/for_human/verdict. Host finalization persists the report and a transcript-derived opened_in_full lower bound; agent-result serves it, harvest prints it, land prints unreconciled blocking findings without gating. Human reconciliation stays in the session record and survives later reads. Read-only launch captures changed paths across all repos (including deleted paths, both rename sides, and submodule pins), independent of later fixer sessions. jq, already installed, parses JSON and the output formats; no new dependency. Conservative counting: successful pi read events or Claude Read tool-use/result pairs, default whole-file requests, textual nontruncated results only; unknown/missing evidence yields null, not a fabricated zero. Claude results at the default 2000-line ceiling are excluded. Tests cover both harnesses, partial/failed/truncated/shell reads, immutable denominator, deleted/renamed paths, report parsing, reconciliation persistence and evidence surviving removal of its inputs. Verified: agent-review, agent-runs and agent-quadlet tests, shell syntax and diff checks, core gate green; doctor remains at baseline 3 warnings/0 violations. LSP checks unavailable/unsupported. Container-backed agent-land test cannot run: podman is absent per sandbox rules. No image changes.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf
[ ] Extract the sandbox into its own repo =sandbox-extract $Decided 2026-09-25 after evaluate-agent-runners: we write our own. Do after sandbox-driver-split, once the runner, harness adapters, harvest and land contain no ClearHead reference; grep for it as the check. The ClearHead driver stays on the ClearHead side. Extraction surfaces the remaining platform assumptions: the agents directory mount, the host pi paths, agent branch naming, and harvest assuming submodules. Proof of done: a run, harvest and land on a second repo that has no .clearhead directory.  ADDED 2026-09-29: harness tooling must leave the target repo's image. Today the platform Containerfile installs claude-code and pi, which a general runner cannot require of every repo; the runner adds its own harness layer over the repo's toolchain image, and owns the harness version pins there. The pending pi bump belongs to that layer: pi 0.99.1 (released 2026-09-29) has gpt-6.1-sol under the same openai-codex provider id, but 0.85.1 to 0.99.1 spans breaking changes, so bump it with a smoke run.  PREPARED 2026-09-30 after sandbox-driver-split: the runner no longer mentions ClearHead (grep leaves only two readers of pre-split records in agent-status and agent-harvest). NEEDS the human first on two points, ask before coding: port to Rust or Babashka or move the POSIX sh as is (about 900 lines, working); and the repo's name and whether platform uses it as a submodule or from PATH. Runner files to move: scripts/agent-new, agent-run, agent-result, agent-reconcile, agent-stop, agent-status, agent-harvest, agent-land, scripts/lib/agent-quadlet.sh and agent-runs.sh, both test files, agents/session, agents/models.env. Stays in platform: scripts/clearhead-work, .sandbox/, docs/overnight-runbook.md. Platform assumptions still in the runner, from reading the code: 1) every script finds the target repo as the parent of its own directory; it must become the git toplevel of the caller's cwd. 2) agent-new snapshots the target repo's agents/ directory; session and models.env must come from the tool's own install. 3) the root Containerfile installs claude-code and pi; split it into the repo's toolchain image and a harness layer the runner builds on top. 4) agent-run mounts Rust-only cache volumes agent-cargo and agent-target, and the image sets CARGO_TARGET_DIR; a repo should declare its own cache volumes under .sandbox. 5) agent-new reads the host's pi settings unconditionally, so a host without pi cannot create a workspace. 6) agent-land defaults its gate to scripts/validate-pinned and has submodule pin logic; the gate should come from the repo, and landing a repo with no submodules is unverified. 7) agent-harvest labels the root repo platform. 8) limits are fixed at 8 CPUs and 16G in agent-run and the unit renderer. Proof of done stays: a session, harvest and land on a second repo with no .clearhead directory. DECIDED 2026-09-30 with the human: move the POSIX sh as is, no port (revisit only when the sh hurts). It lives in a sibling repo of platform (~/Products/<name>), used from PATH, not as a submodule. Name: the human suggested agent-jobs; settle it when the repo is created. Sequence: after sandbox-premerge-gate and one real multi-action work run with a cross-vendor review, so the interface is exercised before it is frozen. Name settled 2026-09-30: agent-sandbox, at ~/Products/agent-sandbox.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T16:13 <01a0dab3-2d95-7d53-a65e-cb88d090e1bd <01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf #01a0dad8-d9ba-79f1-8a9f-b5b68c0e3b0a
[ ] Automatically reconcile detached Quadlet runs =sandbox-auto-reconcile $Follow-up to landed run 20260925-215528. On the host, terminating the foreground systemctl start --wait caller left the Quadlet active with Result=success and ExecMainCode=0. The landed launcher correctly preserves the active unit and manifest; scripts/agent-status flags RECONCILE after it stops and scripts/agent-reconcile <id> is safe, idempotent manual recovery. For unattended operation, make terminal reconciliation automatic without a shell polling supervisor: use a systemd-owned completion/reaper mechanism or another bounded durable trigger. Preserve Git-only output, run isolation, no automatic mutation retry, and the explicit manual command. Acceptance: interrupt the launcher during a bounded no-model unit; the unit continues; when it exits the manifest reaches the correct terminal outcome and unit file is cleaned without operator intervention. Prove success, failure, timeout, OOM and manual stop retain accurate status; record recovery evidence in the manifest. Do not conflate this with the separate interactive model-control-channel design. Sequence first for unattended safety: systemd can outlive the launcher, but terminal manifest cleanup must not depend on an operator remembering the manual command. Preserve agent-reconcile as recovery and test a killed launcher against a real bounded unit.  NOTE 2026-09-30, after sandbox-driver-split, re-read before working: launchers no longer wait by default, and a stopped session is now finalized lazily by whoever touches the workspace next: scripts/agent-result, the next scripts/agent-run --in, and scripts/agent-harvest all call agent-reconcile, which works per session (unit agent-WORKSPACE-N, record sessions/N.json). What is left of this action is reconciliation when nobody calls anything, the systemd-owned trigger. Rewrite the acceptance steps for sessions: the unit and record names above replace the per-run ones.$ !1 ^2026-09-25T22:40 #01a0dc3a-f71b-7f82-97be-829c00623a8e
[=] Define a read-only planner and proposal handoff =sandbox-planner-contract $NEEDS DECISION: who approves and applies planner-proposed ClearHead action/charter changes—the human, a deliberating council, or a bounded driver policy? A genuinely read-only planner cannot directly mutate .clearhead; have it inspect the action, charter, specs, code and prior run evidence, validate feasibility, and emit a structured proposal with candidate CLI mutations, acceptance checks, dependencies, unknowns and stop/ask points. A distinct authorized materializer applies accepted changes through the ClearHead CLI and commits them; proposals are evidence, not authority. Alternative: grant narrowly scoped .clearhead write access in an isolated clone, but then this is a writer role, not read-only. Keep this ClearHead-specific policy in the driver, while the sandbox only hosts a generic read-only run kind and artifact. Preserve human authority over consequential decisions and avoid making a planner the top-down dispatcher. Decide the approval boundary before implementing.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:45 #01a0dc3f-bb67-76d2-a4ad-920ae1f90b3a
[=] Design interruptible interactive agent sessions =sandbox-interactive-control $NEEDS DECISION: what authenticated local input/control channel may interrupt an active sandbox model session without weakening the Git-only code-output boundary? Quadlet attachment alone cannot add a turn to current headless pi -p/Claude -p sessions. Specify cancellation versus checkpoint/resume, transport (persistent RPC or PTY relay), audit of human interventions, unit/manifest identity, credential isolation, failure/reconnect behavior, and whether a controller may write only prompts or broader tools. Preserve resource caps and independent review. This is a separate mode from unattended one-turn work; do not change the active runner until the authority and security contract is approved.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:45 #01a0dc3f-bb8e-7192-8113-f2b84efa412e
[ ] Pilot a lower-cost worker model on decided work =sandbox-cheap-worker-pilot $After the planner proposal contract and first-class cross-vendor review run exist, pilot a cheaper worker route such as DeepSeek via the sandbox harness adapter on a few bounded, already-decided actions in isolated Quadlets. Strong planning and independent review are advisory gates, not automatic authority; no self-review. Keep the same spend, wall-time and commit/gate contracts. Compare cost and elapsed time per accepted landed action, retries, stop/NEEDS DECISION rate and reviewer effort against a stronger worker baseline; cheap tokens alone are not the success metric. Record model/provider per session and let the driver select roles by policy without hard-coding ClearHead or a model into the generic sandbox. Do not make the cheap model the default for broad multi-seam or high-risk tasks without evidence.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:46 <01a0dc3f-bb67-76d2-a4ad-920ae1f90b3a <01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf #01a0dc3f-e6ce-7cd3-802a-473ab21aa73a
[ ] Record the human's verdict on each landing $DECIDED 2026-09-30: the feedback loop needs the human's call next to the reviewer's, or no reviewer can be calibrated. Per landing, store in the workspace manifest {agrees_with_review, overruled: \[finding], missed: \[what the human found that the review did not], note}. One line per landing; the orchestrator may record it on the human's word. This is the data that lets usage grow: miss rate and false-alarm rate per reviewer and per kind of change decide where human review can get lighter. After sandbox-cross-vendor-review.$ !2 ^2026-09-30T13:30 <sandbox-cross-vendor-review #01a0f402-89bf-74e2-8d1c-ba7689ddee66
